Package: tt-toplike
Architecture: amd64
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: tt-smi, tenstorrent-dkms, curl
Suggests: tt-toplike-app, tenstorrent-tools
Description: Real-time hardware monitoring TUI for Tenstorrent silicon
 tt-toplike is a terminal-based monitoring tool for Tenstorrent hardware
 (Grayskull, Wormhole, Blackhole). Each view draws the same live telemetry
 in a different way:
 .
  - insights: per-chip panels with power, temperature, DDR status and processes
  - arcade: split-screen starfield, castle, defrag and flow in one view
  - castle, starfield, flow, defrag: single-view takes on the memory hierarchy
    and the Tensix cores
  - saga: a party of heroes, one per chip, questing through the machine's
    signals
  - wafer waffler: the board and its dies in isometric 3D
  - inference, training and gallery views for running models and recordings
  - your own: a view can be added as a Rust folder or as a YAML file
    (tt-toplike viz new <name> --yaml)
 .
 The signal bus records and replays what tt-toplike sees (--bus-dir,
 --bus-inspect), and --host runs every view on a machine with no Tenstorrent
 hardware.
 .
 Uses safe, non-invasive backends by default. Direct hardware access via
 Luwen (PCI BAR0) is available only via --backend luwen and should never
 be used while workloads are running.
 .
 Backend auto-detection order:
  - hybrid: sysfs real-time + tt-smi SMBUS enrichment (preferred)
  - sysfs:  Linux hwmon sensors — zero interference, no permissions needed
  - json:   tt-smi subprocess (requires tt-smi package from TT PPA)
  - mock:   simulated telemetry, no hardware required

Package: tt-toplike-app
Architecture: amd64
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, tt-toplike
Recommends: libvulkan1 | libgl1
Description: Native window host for tt-toplike (PTY terminal)
 tt-toplike-app opens a native graphical window (eframe/egui) that hosts
 tt-toplike-tui inside a PTY. The TUI runs as a child process; its VT output
 is parsed and rendered by a custom VT100 state machine so the full-colour,
 animated TUI visualizations appear in a proper desktop window.
 .
 Keyboard input is forwarded to the PTY child as ANSI escape sequences.
 The window resizes the PTY on geometry changes so the TUI always fills the
 available space.
 .
 Requires a graphical display (Wayland or X11) and Vulkan or OpenGL.
 Not suitable for headless / server installs.
